+++
title: Achaemenid Empire
description: Ancient Iranian empire, 550–330 BC
hook: Cyrus the Great founded it in 550 BC; at its peak it covered roughly 5.5
  million square kilometres, the largest empire of its time. In 330 BC it was
  conquered in its entirety by Alexander the Great.
standfirst: An ancient Iranian empire, founded in 550 BC and based in the
  Iranian plateau, that stretched from the Balkans and Cyrenaica in the west
  to the Indus Valley in the east. It is recognized for its centralized
  bureaucracy, its multicultural policy and religious tolerance, especially
  under Cyrus, and for infrastructure such as the Royal Road.
key-facts:
  - name: Ran for
    value: 550–330 BC
    from: "description: Ancient Iranian empire, 550–330 BC"
  - name: Size in 500 BC
    value: 5,500,000 km2 (2,100,000 sq mi)
    from: |-
      area:
          500-bc: 5,500,000 km2 (2,100,000 sq mi)
  - name: People in 500 BC
    value: 17–35 million
    from: |-
      population:
          500-bc: 17–35 million
  - name: Capitals
    value: Babylon; Pasargadae (Cyrus the Great); Ecbatana (ceremonial); Susa
      (Darius the Great); Persepolis (ceremonial)
    from: |-
      capital:
          - Babylon
          - Pasargadae (Cyrus the Great)
          - Ecbatana (ceremonial)
          - Susa (Darius the Great)
          - Persepolis (ceremonial)
  - name: What the ruler was called
    value: King of Kings
    from: "office-held-by-head-of-state: King of Kings"
  - name: Money
    value: The Daric and the siglos
    from: |-
      currency:
            - Daric
            - siglos
pull-quotes:
  - text: "The Persian nation contains a number of tribes as listed here. \\... :
      the Pasargadae, Maraphii, and Maspii, upon which all the other tribes
      are dependent. Of these, the Pasargadae are the most distinguished; they
      contain the clan of the Achaemenids from which spring the Perseid
      kings."
    attribution: Herodotus, Histories 1.101 & 125
    section: origin-of-the-achaemenid-dynasty
  - text: there is nothing in the world that travels faster than these Persian
      couriers. Neither snow, nor rain, nor heat, nor gloom of night stays
      these courageous couriers from the swift completion of their appointed
      rounds.
    attribution: Herodotus, on the relays of mounted couriers
    section: transportation-and-communication
  - text: "[the Persians] have no images of the gods, no temples nor altars, and
      consider the use of them a sign of folly. This comes, I think, from
      their not believing the gods to have the same nature with men, as the
      Greeks imagine."
    attribution: Herodotus, writing during the reign of Artaxerxes I and Darius II
    section: religion
  - text: Yaka timber was brought from Gandara and from Carmania. The gold was
      brought from Sardis and from Bactria \... the precious stone
      lapis-lazuli and carnelian \... was brought from Sogdiana. The turquoise
      from Chorasmia, the silver and ebony from Egypt, the ornamentation from
      Ionia, the ivory from Ethiopia and from Sindh and from Arachosia.
    attribution: Darius the Great, DSf inscription
    section: architecture-and-art
  - text: nothing has been established with certainty at the present time, given
      the available evidence
    attribution: Pierre Briant, on whether Darius fabricated the impostor Bardiya
    section: 520s-bc
  - text: The Persian Empire is an empire in the modern sense—like that which
      existed in Germany, and the great imperial realm under the sway of
      Napoleon; for we find it consisting of a number of states, which are
      indeed dependent, but which have retained their own individuality, their
      manners, and laws.
    attribution: Georg W. F. Hegel, The Philosophy of History
    section: legacy
timeline:
  - when: Around 850 BC
    what: The nomadic people who began the empire call themselves the Parsa, and
      their constantly shifting territory Parsua.
    from: Around 850 BC the original nomadic people who began the empire called
      themselves the Parsa and their constantly shifting territory Parsua, for
      the most part localized around Persis.
  - when: 705 BC
    what: Achaemenes, first ruler of the Achaemenid kingdom and founder of the
      dynasty — attested to only by the Behistun Inscription.
    from: First ruler of the Achaemenid kingdom and founder of the dynasty.
      Attested to only by the Behistun Inscription. ... 705 BC
  - when: 553 BC
    what: Cyrus revolts against the Median Empire.
    from: Cyrus revolted against the Median Empire in 553 BC, and in 550 BC
      succeeded in defeating the Medes, capturing Astyages and taking the
      Median capital city of Ecbatana.
  - when: 550 BC
    what: "Succession to Media: Cyrus defeats the Medes and takes Ecbatana."
    from: "succession-to-media: 550 BC"
  - when: 547 BC
    what: Conquest of Lydia. (The prose puts the fall of the Lydian Kingdom in 546
      BC.)
    from: "conquest-of-lydia: 547 BC"
  - when: 539 BC
    what: Conquest of Babylonia. Cyrus wins at Opis in October, takes Sippar
      without a fight, then captures Babylon on 12 October.
    from: "conquest-of-babylonia: 539 BC ... In October 539 BC, Cyrus won a battle
      against the Babylonians at Opis, then took Sippar without a fight before
      finally capturing the city of Babylon on 12 October, where the
      Babylonian king Nabonidus was taken prisoner."
  - when: 535–518 BC
    what: Conquest of the Indus Valley.
    from: "conquest-of-the-indus-valley: 535–518 BC"
  - when: 525 BC
    what: Conquest of Egypt.
    from: "conquest-of-egypt: 525 BC"
  - when: 522 BC
    what: Gaumata, having ruled for seven months according to the Behistun
      inscription, is overthrown by Darius the Great.
    from: According to the Behistun inscription, Gaumata ruled for seven months
      before being overthrown in 522 BC by Darius the Great
  - when: 513 BC
    what: Invasion of Scythia.
    from: "invasion-of-scythia: 513 BC"
  - when: 499–449 BC
    what: The Greco-Persian Wars.
    from: "greco-persian-wars: 499–449 BC"
  - when: 484 BC
    what: Babylonian revolts.
    from: "babylonian-revolts: 484 BC"
  - when: 401 BC
    what: Cyrus the Younger is stopped and killed at Cunaxa by the royal army of
      Artaxerxes II.
    from: The army of Cyrus was stopped by the royal Persian army of Artaxerxes II
      at Cunaxa in 401 BC, where Cyrus was killed.
  - when: 395–387 BC
    what: The Corinthian War.
    from: "corinthian-war: 395–387 BC"
  - when: 372–362 BC
    what: The Great Satraps' Revolt.
    from: "great-satraps-revolt: 372–362 BC"
  - when: 343 BC
    what: Reconquest of Egypt.
    from: "reconquest-of-egypt: 343 BC"
  - when: 330 BC
    what: Fall to Macedonia. Persepolis surrenders and is destroyed by fire.
    from: "fall-to-macedonia: 330 BC ... Afterwards, he marched on Susa and
      Persepolis which surrendered and was destroyed by fire in early 330 BC."
  - when: 323 BC
    what: Alexander dies and his empire is divided among his generals, the
      Diadochi.
    from: Upon Alexander's death in 323 BC, his empire was divided among his
      generals, the Diadochi, resulting in a number of smaller states.
  - when: 224 AD
    what: Ardaxšir V defeats the last legitimate Parthian king and is crowned at
      Ctesiphon as Ardaxšir I, first king of the Sasanian Empire.
    from: Ardaxšir (Artaxerxes) V, defeated the last legitimate Parthian king,
      Artabanos V in 224 AD, and was crowned at Ctesiphon as Ardaxšir I
      (Ardashir I), šāhanšāh ī Ērān, becoming the first king of the new
      Sasanian Empire.
