glossary:
  - term: satrapy
    gloss: An administrative unit of the empire, usually organized on a
      geographical basis; at differing times there were between 20 and 30 of
      them.
    from: A satrapy was an administrative unit, usually organized on a
      geographical basis. ... At differing times, there were between 20 and 30
      satrapies.
  - term: satrap
    gloss: The governor who administered a satrapy, with a 'general' supervising
      military recruitment and a 'state secretary' keeping the records, both
      reporting to him and to the central government.
    from: A 'satrap' (governor) was the governor who administered the region, a
      'general' supervised military recruitment and ensured order, and a
      'state secretary' kept the official records. The general and the state
      secretary reported directly to the satrap as well as the central
      government.
  - term: Immortals
    gloss: Heavy infantry, in Herodotus's description, kept constantly at a
      strength of exactly 10,000 men — he claimed the name came from the
      custom of immediately replacing every killed, seriously wounded or sick
      member.
    from: The Immortals were described by Herodotus as being heavy infantry, led
      by Hydarnes II, that were kept constantly at a strength of exactly
      10,000 men. He claimed that the unit's name stemmed from the custom that
      every killed, seriously wounded, or sick member was immediately replaced
      with a new one, maintaining the numbers and cohesion of the unit.
  - term: Sparabara
    gloss: Persian infantry, believed to have formed a shield wall and used
      two-metre-long spears to protect more vulnerable troops such as archers;
      not much is known about them today.
    from: Although not much is known about them today, it is believed that they
      were the backbone of the Persian army who formed a shield wall and used
      their two-metre-long spears to protect more vulnerable troops such as
      archers from the enemy.
  - term: daric
    gloss: The Persian gold coin — the first — which with the silver siglos
      introduced the bimetallic monetary standard of the Achaemenids.
    from: The Persian daric was the first gold coin which, along with a similar
      silver coin, the siglos, introduced the bimetallic monetary standard of
      the Achaemenids, which has continued until today.
  - term: Chapar Khaneh
    gloss: The relay stations on which Cyrus's postal system throughout the empire
      was based.
    from: Cyrus also formed an innovative postal system throughout the empire,
      based on several relay stations called Chapar Khaneh.
  - term: angarium
    gloss: The relays of mounted couriers on the royal highway, which could reach
      the remotest of areas in fifteen days.
    from: The relays of mounted couriers (the angarium) could reach the remotest
      of areas in fifteen days.
  - term: Frataraka
    gloss: The dynasty of later Persian rulers known to have acted as
      representatives of the Seleucids in the region of Fārs; the title
      disappears from their coins before the title mlk, king, appears.
    from: Several later Persian rulers, forming the Frataraka dynasty, are known
      to have acted as representatives of the Seleucids in the region of Fārs.
      ... The earlier title prtrk' zy alhaya (Frataraka) had disappeared.
      Under Dārēv I (Darios I) however, the new title of mlk, or king,
      appeared
further:
  - slug: pasargadae
    why: Cyrus's capital, and where his tomb was built — Alexander went there from
      Persepolis to see it, found it broken into and looted, and put the Magi
      on trial.
  - slug: persepolis
    why: "The ceremonial capital Darius expanded and Artaxerxes II extended again:
      the grand palace, the administrative archives of more than 10,000
      tablets, and the tombs of Artaxerxes II and III."
  - slug: alexander-the-great
    why: He conquered the empire in its entirety in 330 BC and generally kept its
      administrative structure, which has led some scholars to dub him "the
      last of the Achaemenids".
anchors:
  hook: was an ancient Iranian empire founded by Cyrus the Great of the
    Achaemenid dynasty in 550 BC. At peak, its territorial extent was roughly
    5.5 million square kilometres (2.1 million square miles), making it the
    largest empire of its time. ... In 330 BC, amidst a Greek military
    campaign that began in 336 BC, the Achaemenid Empire was conquered in its
    entirety by Alexander the Great, who annexed it to his Macedonian Empire.
  standfirst: was an ancient Iranian empire founded by Cyrus the Great of the
    Achaemenid dynasty in 550 BC. ... Based in the Iranian plateau, it
    stretched from the Balkans and Cyrenaica in the west to the Indus Valley
    in the east, including Anatolia, Cyprus, Mesopotamia, the Levant, the
    South Caucasus, parts of Eastern Arabia, and large parts of Central Asia.
    ... In the modern era, the Achaemenid Empire has been recognized for its
    centralized bureaucracy and administration; its multicultural policy and
    religious tolerance, especially under Cyrus; its complex infrastructure
    projects, such as the Royal Road and an organized postal system
